; A CBOR tag wrapping a homogeneous table (`#6.n({ * k => v })`). The rule lowers to a TRANSPARENT
; map alias (`pub type TaggedTable = BTreeMap<String, u64>;`) with the tag riding the alias, so every
; EMBED site owns the tag: `holder`'s serialize writes `write_tag(11)` then the map, and its
; deserialize rejects a wrong tag with `DeserializeFailure::TagMismatch`. The alias itself mints no
; codec, so `TaggedTable`'s standalone `to/from_cbor_bytes` are `BTreeMap`'s and drop the tag — the
; one-type-two-wire-forms class ledgered in cddl-matrix/ROADMAP.md § Findings ("A tagged rule body
; that stays a transparent alias drops the tag from its own standalone codec"), which this fixture
; records rather than fixes. Under `--wasm=true` the emitted wasm
; wrapper is accessors-only and DELEGATES serialization to that rust type, so it carries no tag logic
; of its own — pinning that the wasm wrapper generates and compiles for a tagged table (regression:
; `codegen_table_type` used to `assert!(tag.is_none())` and panic on exactly this shape).
; The value type is `uint` (not `int`) on purpose: an `int`-valued table would drag in the separate,
; still-unresolved undefined-`Int` extern class, so `uint` keeps this fixture pinning ONLY the tag case.
; `holder` embeds it so the member/embed role compiles alongside the standalone rule.
tagged_table = #6.11({ * tstr => uint })
holder = [payload: tagged_table]
